nd model performance.\n\n### Step 1: Hydrology_Elevation_Ratio\npython\n# Hydrology_Elevation_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of the absolute vertical distance to hydrology to elevation.\n# Usefulness: Captures relative proximity of surface elevation above/below water features. Areas closer to surface water (vertically normalized) may support certain forest types.\ndf['Hydrology_Elevation_Ratio'] = abs(df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']) / (df['Elevation'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n### Step 2: Fire_Proximity_Weight\npython\n# Fire_Proximity_Weight\n# Feature description: Weight the horizontal distance to fire points by elevation.\n# Usefulness: Areas farther from fire points but at different elevations may respond uniquely to fire patterns, influencing forest resilience and growth.\ndf['Fire_Proximity_Weight'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'] / (df['Elevation'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n### Step 3: Wilderness_Soil_Diversity\npython\n# Wilderness_Soil_Diversity\n# Feature description: Combine all wilderness area indicators and soil type diversity into a single metric.\n# Usefulness: Forest type can be influenced by the intersection of regional wilderness zones and the variety of soil types in those regions. This creates a consolidated feature to capture these dependencies.\ndf['Wilderness_Soil_Diversity'] = df[['Wilderness_Area1', 'Wilderness_Area2', 'Wilderness_Area3', 'Wilderness_Area4']].sum(axis=1) + df[[col for col in df.columns if 'Soil_Type' in col]].sum(axis=1)\nend\n\n### Drop redundant and low-value columns:\n1. Soil_Type indicators with near-zero variance or occurrence.\n2. Wilderness_Area4 due to sparsity across the dataset.\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Wilderness_Area4 is dropped\n# Wilderness_Area4 contains mostly zero values, contributing negligible information to the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['Wilderness_Area4'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type8 is dropped\n# Soil_Type8 shows near-zero occurrence (Mean = 0.0