zontal_Distance_To_Hydrology, and Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points. This captures interaction effects of proximity to infrastructure, water, and fire points.\n# Usefulness: Interactions between human infrastructure, water sources, and fire proximity can significantly influence forest cover, as these factors govern both environmental and anthropogenic impacts.\ndf['Proximity_Interaction'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] * df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] * df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points']\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type28 is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type28 has near-zero mean and variance (Mean = 0.0016, Std = 0.0399), contributing little or no predictive power to the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type28'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type8 is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type8 has minimal occurrence (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186) and lacks variability, making it ineffective for predicting forest cover types.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type36 is dropped\n# Reason: Similar to other low-variance soil types, Soil_Type36 has an extremely small mean (Mean = 0.0001, Std = 0.0103) and is unlikely to provide meaningful information for predictions.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend", "python\n# Feature: Elevation_To_Hydrology_Ratio\n# Feature description: Compute the ratio of Elevation to the absolute value of Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology. This evaluates the relative vertical positioning of a point above surface water.\n# Usefulness: Captures hydrological influence on vegetation by linking elevation heights to proximity to water sources, which can impact soil moisture and forest cover type.\ndf['Elevation_To_Hydrology_Ratio'] = df['Elevation'] / (abs(df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']) + 1e-6)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: Combined_Hillshade_Index\n# Feature description: Calculate the weighted average of Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, and Hillshade_3pm to summarize overall sunlight exposure.\n# Usefulness: Sunlight exposure affects vegetation growth patterns and forest composition. This feature combines sunlight exposure across the day for a holistic measure.\ndf['Combined_Hillshade_Index'] = (0.3 * df['Hillshade_9am'] + 0.4 * df['Hillshade_Noon'] + 0.3 * df['Hillshade_3pm'])\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: Proximity_Factor\n# Feature description: Compute the geometric mean of Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways, Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points, and Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology.\n# Usefulness: This captures a combined factor of proximity to roads, fire points, and hydrology, which could influence forest cover type by representing human and environmental impact.\ndf['Proximity_Factor'] = (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] * \n                          df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'] * \n                          df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'])  (1/3)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type8 is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type8 has minimal variability (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186) and negligible occurrence, contributing no predictive value.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type36 is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type36 shows near-zero occurrence (Mean = 0.0001, Std = 0.0103), thus offering no useful information for predictions.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type28 is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type28 has very low variability (Mean = 0.0016, Std = 0.0399) and is unlikely to influence predictions of forest cover type significantly.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type28'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nEach of these steps thoughtfully enhances feature representation or removes irrelevant columns, ensuring the dataset remains concise and informative for modeling forest cover types.", "python\n# Feature: Vertical_Above_Hydrology\n# Feature description: Calculate the absolute vertical distance of a point above or below hydrology.\n# Usefulness: Differentiates points that are above or below the nearest water body, which could influence forest type due to soil drainage or water availability.\ndf['Vertical_Above_Hydrology'] = abs(df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology'])\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: Distance_Factor\n# Feature description: Calculate the geometric mean of Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology and Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways.\n# Usefulness: Combines environmental (water proximity) and anthropogenic (road proximity) influences in a single metric, which may impact forest coverage patterns more effectively than standalone features.\ndf['Distance_Factor'] = (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] * df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'])  (1/2)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: Hillshade_Skew\n# Feature description: Calculate the difference between Hillshade_Noon and the average of Hillshade_9am and Hillshade_3pm to capture the midday sunlight's deviation.\n# Usefulness: This skew metric captures light conditions during the most intense sunlight hours, potentially identifying areas with environmental conditions favoring certain forest types.\ndf['Hillshade_Skew'] = df['Hillshade_Noon'] - ((df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']) / 2)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type8' is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type8 has minimal occurrence (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186), offering negligible discriminative power.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type36' is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type36 shows near-zero mean (Mean = 0.0001, Std = 0.0103) and no variability, making it irrelevant for predictions.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type25' is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type25 has extremely low variability (Mean = 0.0008, Std = 0.0287) and negligible impact on predicting forest cover types