rall sunlight exposure across the day.\n# Usefulness: Forest growth and vegetation vary based on the amount of sunlight an area receives over the day. This feature simplifies the representation of light intensity while retaining its overall impact.\ndf['Combined_Sunlight_Avg'] = (df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_Noon'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']) / 3\nend\n\n### Step 3: Hydrology_Distance_Slope_Ratio\npython\n# Hydrology_Distance_Slope_Ratio\n# Feature description: Compute the ratio of Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology to Slope, gauging closeness to water sources relative to terrain steepness.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures the interplay between slope and water proximity, which influences water retention, erosion, and vegetation distribution in the forest.\ndf['Hydrology_Distance_Slope_Ratio'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] / (df['Slope'] + 1e-6)  # Add small value for safety.\nend\n\n### Dropping Redundant Columns\n#### Column: Soil_Type3\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type3 is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type3 has minimal variance with a very low mean and standard deviation, contributing negligible predictive value.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type3'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type25\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type25 is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type25 shows near-zero variance and negligible occurrence, making it uninformative for the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type25'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type17\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type17 is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type17 has very low variability, providing limited value to improve the predictive power of the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type17'], inplace=True)\nend  \n\nThese steps aim to generate meaningful transformations to better capture the relationships between features and their ecological and spatial significance while removing uninformative features to streamline model training. Combined, these enhancements aim to improve pre