rall sunlight exposure across the day.\n# Usefulness: Forest growth and vegetation vary based on the amount of sunlight an area receives over the day. This feature simplifies the representation of light intensity while retaining its overall impact.\ndf['Combined_Sunlight_Avg'] = (df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_Noon'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']) / 3\nend\n\n### Step 3: Hydrology_Distance_Slope_Ratio\npython\n# Hydrology_Distance_Slope_Ratio\n# Feature description: Compute the ratio of Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology to Slope, gauging closeness to water sources relative to terrain steepness.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures the interplay between slope and water proximity, which influences water retention, erosion, and vegetation distribution in the forest.\ndf['Hydrology_Distance_Slope_Ratio'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] / (df['Slope'] + 1e-6)  # Add small value for safety.\nend\n\n### Dropping Redundant Columns\n#### Column: Soil_Type3\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type3 is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type3 has minimal variance with a very low mean and standard deviation, contributing negligible predictive value.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type3'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type25\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type25 is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type25 shows near-zero variance and negligible occurrence, making it uninformative for the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type25'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type17\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type17 is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type17 has very low variability, providing limited value to improve the predictive power of the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type17'], inplace=True)\nend  \n\nThese steps aim to generate meaningful transformations to better capture the relationships between features and their ecological and spatial significance while removing uninformative features to streamline model training. Combined, these enhancements aim to improve prediction performance.", "Here are three simple, direct, and intuitive feature engineering steps to improve predictions for forest cover type (1 or 2), along with explanations for dropping redundant columns:\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Distance_To_Hydrology_Combined\npython\n# Distance_To_Hydrology_Combined\n# Feature description: Computes the Euclidean distance to the nearest hydrology feature using both horizontal and vertical distances.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures combined proximity to surface water sources, which impacts soil moisture, vegetation growth, and forest cover types. A single unified metric is more interpretable and effective than separate horizontal and vertical distances.\ndf['Distance_To_Hydrology_Combined'] = (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology']2 + df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']2)0.5\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Elevation_To_Slope_Ratio\npython\n# Elevation_To_Slope_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculates the ratio of Elevation to Slope to capture terrain ruggedness.\n# Usefulness: This feature combines elevation and steepness to highlight terrain variability. Rugged terrain often correlates with distinct water flow, erosion, and vegetation patterns—important factors influencing forest cover types.\ndf['Elevation_To_Slope_Ratio'] = df['Elevation'] / (df['Slope'] + 1e-6)  # Adding small constant to prevent division by zero\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Combined_Hillshade_Average\npython\n# Combined_Hillshade_Average\n# Feature description: Calculates the average of Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, and Hillshade_3pm to capture overall sunlight exposure throughout the day.\n# Usefulness: Forest types are influenced by daily sunlight exposure. This feature simplifies the representation of light intensity while retaining its predictive power for vegetation growth and microclimate conditions.\ndf['Combined_Hillshade_Average'] = (df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_Noon'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']) / 3\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Redundant Columns:\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type36\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type36 is dropped\n# Soil_Type36 has a near-zero mean (Mean = 0.0001, Std = 0.0103) and negligible variance, making it irrelevant for predicting forest cover types.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type28\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type28 is dropped\n# Soil_Type28 has very low variability (Mean = 0.0016, Std = 0.0399) and minimal impact on predicting the target variable.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type28'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type8\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type8 is dropped\n# Soil_Type8 has minimal occurrence (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186), contributing negligible predictive value to the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Summary:\n1. Added Features:\n   - Distance_To_Hydrology_Combined: Unified representation of hydrology distances.\n   - Elevation_To_Slope_Ratio: Terrain ruggedness, combining elevation and slope.\n   - Combined_Hillshade_Average: Simplified sunlight exposure throughout the day.\n\n2. Dropped Columns:\n   - Removed low-variance soil types (Soil_Type36, Soil_Type28, Soil_Type8) to simplify the dataset and focus on informative features.\n\nThis approach is simple, direct, and leverages existing features to generate meaningful interactions and relationships while streamlining the dataset."]###,
