2026-05-13 13:18:57,344 - INFO - Selected state: ("Here are three possible next steps to generate useful features, aiming to improve the downstream task of predicting forest cover type (1 or 2). The reasoning behind each is grounded in extracting meaningful transformations of the existing features.\n\n### Step 1: Create Elevation_diff_Hydrology\npython\n# Elevation_diff_Hydrology\n# Calculates the absolute difference between Elevation and Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology.\n# Usefulness: This captures how far above or below surface water a location lies, which may influence vegetation and forest cover type.\ndf['Elevation_diff_Hydrology'] = abs(df['Elevation'] - df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology'])\nend\n\n### Step 2: Calculate normalized Slope using Z-Score\npython\n# ZScore_Slope\n# Normalizes the slope feature using Z-score transformation.\n# Usefulness: A normalized slope provides information about steepness and helps handle the dataset values better for a machine learning model. Extreme slopes strongly affect erosion, water retention, and soil consistency — all key factors for forest cover.\ndf['ZScore_Slope'] = (df['Slope'] - df['Slope'].mean()) / df['Slope'].std()\nend\n\n### Step 3: Bin Hillshade_9am into categories\npython\n# Hillshade_9am_bin\n# Bins the Hillshade_9am values into categories such as 'low', 'medium', and 'high' using predefined thresholds.\n# Usefulness: Binning simplifies the continuous Hillshade_9am data, making it easier to identify sunlight patterns which influence vegetation and indirectly forest cover type.\nbins = [0, 150, 200, 255]\nlabels = ['low', 'medium', 'high']\ndf['Hillshade_9am_bin'] = pd.cut(df['Hillshade_9am'], bins=bins, labels=labels)\nend\n\nNow let's drop some redundant columns or irrelevant information:\n\n### Drop Plain Soil Columns (All-Zeros):\npython\n# Dropping Soil_Type1, Soil_Type5, Soil_Type14, Soil_Type15, and Soil_Type37 as these are always zero across all data points.\n# Explanation: These columns are uninformative as they provide no variability and hence do not contribute to the model's predictions.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type1', 'Soil_Type5', 'Soil_Type14', 'Soil_Type15', 'Soil_Type37'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nEach update focuses on enhancing the dataset with transformations to derive meaningful relationships between the features. These added features align with the dataset's context and cartographic variables while retaining simplicity and intuitiveness.", "Here are the best possible next steps for generating new features based on the task requirements:\n\n### Step 1: Ratio of Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways and Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points\npython\n# Road_Fire_Ratio\n# Calculates the ratio of distance to nearest roadway and distance to nearest wildfire ignition point.\n# Usefulness: This captures the relative positioning of roadways and fire points, which can influence human activity and accessibility. Areas closer to roads may have different forest management practices, while areas nearer fire points may have vegetation conducive to wildfires.\ndf['Road_Fire_Ratio'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\n### Step 2: Aspect features (Sine and Cosine transformation)\npython\n# Aspect_Sine and Aspect_Cosine\n# Transforms the Aspect feature into sine and cosine components to capture directional information of terrain.\n# Usefulness: Aspect influences sunlight exposure and local microclimates. Representing it as sine and cosine components makes the data cyclical and more suitable for machine learning models to capture directional variability.\ndf['Aspect_Sine'] = np.sin(np.radians(df['Aspect']))\ndf['Aspect_Cosine'] = np.cos(np.radians(df['Aspect']))\nend\n\n### Step 3: Elevation normalized across Wilderness Areas\npython\n# Elevation_Normalized_By_Wilderness\n# Normalizes Elevation within each Wilderness Area to capture local terrain variability.\n# Usefulness: Normalizing Elevation within each Wilderness Area makes it possible to account for regional differences in elevation, which may correlate with forest cover type within specific wilderness areas.\ndf['Elevation_Normalized_By_Wilderness'] = df.groupby('Wilderness_Area1')['Elevation'].transform(lambda x: (x - x.mean()) / x.std())\nend\n\n### Drop irrelevant or redundant columns\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type8 is dropped\n# Soil_Type8 is always zero across all data points, providing no variability or predictive power to the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column Wilderness_Area4 is dropped\n# Wilderness_Area4 is largely zero across all data points, implying negligible occurrences in this dataset. Removing it helps reduce noise and sparsity.\ndf.drop(columns=['Wilderness_Area4'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nEach feature engineering step adds meaningful context and enables the downstream model to better understand relationships between cartographic features and forest cover type.", "Here are three possible next steps for feature engineering, carefully designed to provide meaningful improvements to the downstream task of predicting forest cover type (1 or 2). Each step adds intuitive and contextually relevant features or removes redundant columns:\n\n### Step 1: Elevation_To_Roadways_Ratio\npython\n# Elevation_To_Roadways_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Elevation to Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways. This captures how elevation varies relative to proximity to roads, which could influence accessibility and forest management practices.\n# Usefulness: Elevation in relation to roadways could indirectly affect vegetation types by reflecting accessibility challenges or human impact, both of which are important to forest cover type.\ndf['Elevation_To_Roadways_Ratio'] = df['Elevation'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n### Step 2: Combined_Hillshade_Intensity\npython\n# Combined_Hillshade_Intensity\n# Feature description: Compute a simple average of Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, and Hillshade_3pm to measure overall sunlight intensity throughout the day.\n# Usefulness: Forest ecosystems are strongly influenced by daily sunlight exposure. A combined measure helps identify areas with consistent light patterns, which impact vegetation cover and growth.\ndf['Combined_Hillshade_Intensity'] = (df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_Noon'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']) / 3\nend\n\n### Step 3: Soil_Type_Coverage\npython\n# Soil_Type_Coverage\n# Feature description: Sum all Soil_Type indicators to form a new feature representing soil coverage diversity for a location.\n# Usefulness: Locations with higher soil type diversity may have unique vegetation patterns and forest cover types. This feature aggregates soil-related information into a single metric.\ndf['Soil_Type_Coverage'] = df[[col for col in df.columns if 'Soil_Type' in col]].sum(axis=1)\nend\n\n### Drop irrelevant columns: Zero-Variance Wilderness Areas\npython\n# Explanation why the column Wilderness_Area4 is dropped\n# Wilderness_Area4 contains almost all zero values across the dataset, providing no predictive information.\ndf.drop(columns=['Wilderness_Area4'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n### Drop redundant columns: Low-Impact Soil Type Indicators\npython\n# Explanation why the column sets Soil_Type8 and Soil_Type37 are dropped\n# These columns contain no variability or meaningful differences, and do not contribute to distinguishing forest cover types effectively.\ncolumns_to_drop = ['Soil_Type8', 'Soil_Type37']\ndf.drop(columns=columns_to_drop, inplace=True)\nend\n\nEach of these steps introduces intuitive, real-world features and cleans the dataset of unnecessary columns, ensuring the model has high-quality inputs for improved predictions.", "Here are three possible next steps for feature engineering to improve predictions of forest cover type (1 or 2). Each step introduces intuitive features based on the dataset's cartographic attributes and descriptions.\n\n### Step 1: Adjusted_Slope\npython\n# Adjusted_Slope\n# Feature description: Compute the product of Slope and Elevation to capture how steep terrain interacts with elevation.\n# Usefulness: Terrain steepness combined with elevation can indicate erosion patterns, water flow, and vegetation suitability, all of which are factors impacting forest cover.\ndf['Adjusted_Slope'] = df['Slope'] * df['Elevation']\nend\n\n### Step 2: Hydrology_Road_Factor\npython\n# Hydrology_Road_Factor\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology to Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways.\n# Usefulness: This captures the relative accessibility to water sources versus roads, influencing both natural vegetation growth and human forest management practices.\ndf['Hydrology_Road_Factor'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n### Step 3: Firepoint_Elevation_Difference\npython\n# Firepoint_Elevation_Difference\n# Feature description: Compute the absolute difference between Elevation and Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points.\n# Usefulness: This feature measures how elevation contrasts with proximity to fire points, potentially capturing areas prone to wildfires or human intervention.\ndf['Firepoint_Elevation_Difference'] = abs(df['Elevation'] - df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'])\nend\n\n### Dropping Low-Impact and Sparse Columns\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type8 is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type8 has minimal occurrence (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186) and does not contribute meaningful variance to the dataset.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Wilderness_Area4 is dropped\n# Reason: Wilderness_Area4 has almost all zero values (sparsity), providing negligible predictive power for the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['Wilderness_Area4'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nEach feature and column removal step is motivated by its potential to enhance model interpretability, capture meaningful relationships in the data, or reduce noise from redundant information.", "Here are three possible next steps for feature engineering, designed to be simple, direct, and intuitive, while avoiding the mistakes of previous iterations:\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Distance_To_Hydrology_Combined\n\npython\n# Distance_To_Hydrology_Combined\n# Feature description: Computes the Euclidean distance to the nearest hydrology feature using both horizontal and vertical distances.\n# Usefulness: This unified metric captures combined proximity to water sources, which impacts soil moisture, vegetation growth, and forest cover types. A compact representation improves interpretability.\ndf['Distance_To_Hydrology_Combined'] = np.sqrt(df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology']2 + df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']2)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Fire_Road_Interaction\n\npython\n# Fire_Road_Interaction\n# Feature description: Calculates the product of Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points and Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures the interaction between proximity to wildfire ignition points and roads. It represents areas more prone to human impact and wildfire likelihood.\ndf['Fire_Road_Interaction'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'] * df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways']\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Elevation_Difference_Standardized\n\npython\n# Elevation_Difference_Standardized\n# Feature description: Standardizes the Elevation_diff_Hydrology feature by subtracting its mean and dividing by its standard deviation.\n# Usefulness: Standardizing the elevation difference helps in providing a normalized representation of how the terrain's height differs relative to water sources. This avoids potential bias due to scale differences.\ndf['Elevation_Difference_Standardized'] = (df['Elevation_diff_Hydrology'] - df['Elevation_diff_Hydrology'].mean()) / df['Elevation_diff_Hydrology'].std()\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Redundant Columns\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type8\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type8 is dropped\n# Soil_Type8 has minimal variance (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186), contributing negligibly to model predictions. Removing it simplifies the dataset without losing meaningful information.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type36\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type36 is dropped\n# Soil_Type36 has a near-zero mean (Mean = 0.0001, Std = 0.0103) and variance, making it irrelevant for predicting forest cover types.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type25\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type25 is dropped\n# Soil_Type25 shows extremely low variability (Mean = 0.0008, Std = 0.0287), providing no significant contribution to forest cover classification.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type25'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Summary:\n\n1. Added Features:\n   - Distance_To_Hydrology_Combined: Unified hydrology proximity metric.\n   - Fire_Road_Interaction: Captures the combined impact of human infrastructure and wildfire ignition likelihood.\n   - Elevation_Difference_Standardized: Normalized elevation difference to improve representation for learning algorithms.\n\n2. Dropped Columns:\n   - Soil_Type8, Soil_Type36, Soil_Type25: Removed due to negligible variance and lack of predictive contribution.\n\nThis approach enhances feature representation while streamlining irrelevant attributes, ensuring improved model performance with interpretable and meaningful datasets."), with improvements -
