mpute a simple average of Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, and Hillshade_3pm to measure overall sunlight intensity throughout the day.\n# Usefulness: Forest ecosystems are strongly influenced by daily sunlight exposure. A combined measure helps identify areas with consistent light patterns, which impact vegetation cover and growth.\ndf['Combined_Hillshade_Intensity'] = (df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_Noon'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']) / 3\nend\n\n### Step 3: Soil_Type_Coverage\npython\n# Soil_Type_Coverage\n# Feature description: Sum all Soil_Type indicators to form a new feature representing soil coverage diversity for a location.\n# Usefulness: Locations with higher soil type diversity may have unique vegetation patterns and forest cover types. This feature aggregates soil-related information into a single metric.\ndf['Soil_Type_Coverage'] = df[[col for col in df.columns if 'Soil_Type' in col]].sum(axis=1)\nend\n\n### Drop irrelevant columns: Zero-Variance Wilderness Areas\npython\n# Explanation why the column Wilderness_Area4 is dropped\n# Wilderness_Area4 contains almost all zero values across the dataset, providing no predictive information.\ndf.drop(columns=['Wilderness_Area4'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n### Drop redundant columns: Low-Impact Soil Type Indicators\npython\n# Explanation why the column sets Soil_Type8 and Soil_Type37 are dropped\n# These columns contain no variability or meaningful differences, and do not contribute to distinguishing forest cover types effectively.\ncolumns_to_drop = ['Soil_Type8', 'Soil_Type37']\ndf.drop(columns=columns_to_drop, inplace=True)\nend\n\nEach of these steps introduces intuitive, real-world features and cleans the dataset of unnecessary columns, ensuring the model has high-quality inputs for improved predictions.", "Here are three possible next steps for feature engineering to improve predictions of forest cover type (1 or 2). Each step introduces intuitive features based on the dataset's cartographic attributes and descriptions.\n\n### Step 1: Adjusted_Slope\npython\n# Adjusted_Slope\n# Feature description: Compute the product of Slope and Elevation to capture how steep terrain interacts with elevation.\n# Usefulness: Terrain steepness combined with elevation can indicate erosion patterns, water flow, and vegetation suitability, all of which are factors impacting forest cover.\ndf['Adjusted_Slope'] = df['Slope'] * df['Elevation']\nend\n\n### Step 2: Hydrology_Road_Factor\npython\n# Hydrology_Road_Factor\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology to Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways.\n# Usefulness: This captures the relative accessibility to water sources versus roads, influencing both natural vegetation growth and human forest management practices.\ndf['Hydrology_Road_Factor'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n### Step 3: Firepoint_Elevation_Difference\npython\n# Firepoint_Elevation_Difference\n# Feature description: Compute the absolute difference between Elevation and Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points.\n# Usefulness: This feature measures how elevation contrasts with proximity to fire points, potentially capturing areas prone to wildfires or human intervention.\ndf['Firepoint_Elevation_Difference'] = abs(df['Elevation'] - df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'])\nend\n\n### Dropping Low-Impact and Sparse Columns\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type8 is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type8 has minimal occurrence (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186) and does not contribute meaningful variance to the dataset.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Wilderness_Area4 is dropped\n# Reason: Wilderness_Area4 has almost all zero values (sparsity), providing negligible predictive power for the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['Wilderness_Area4'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nEach feature and column removal step is motivated by its potential to enhance model interpretability, capture meaningful relationships in the data, or reduce noise from redundant information.", "Here are three simple, direct, and intuitive steps for feature engineering, designed to improve predictions for forest cover type (1 or 2). Each step introduces meaningful features based on the dataset's attributes and ecological context:\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Elevation_To_Hydrology_3D_Ratio\npython\n# Elevation_To_Hydrology_3D_Ratio\n# Feature description: Compute the ratio of Elevation to the Euclidean distance to the nearest hydrology feature, combining horizontal and vertical distances.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures the relative elevation height compared to proximity to water sources in three dimensions. Higher ratios may correlate with distinct vegetation profiles influenced by terrain and water availability.\ndf['Elevation_To_Hydrology_3D_Ratio'] = df['Elevation'] / (np.sqrt(df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology']2 + df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']2) + 1e-6)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Overall_Sunlight_Intensity\npython\n# Overall_Sunlight_Intensity\n# Feature description: Compute the average of Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, and Hillshade_3pm to measure total daily sunlight exposure.\n# Usefulness: Sunlight exposure drives vegetation growth and forest cover patterns. A consolidated metric simplifies time-based variability and captures cumulative sunlight intensity throughout the day.\ndf['Overall_Sunlight_Intensity'] = (df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_Noon'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']) / 3\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Road_Fire_Proximity_Factor\npython\n# Road_Fire_Proximity_Factor\n# Feature description: Compute the ratio of Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways to Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points, capturing the relative influence of road proximity and fire-prone areas.\n# Usefulness: Areas closer to roads yet further from fire points may exhibit different forest dynamics due to better management and lower wildfire susceptibility. This feature highlights these relationships for predictive modeling.\ndf['Road_Fire_Proximity_Factor'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Redundant Columns\n\n#### Step 1: Soil_Type8\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type8 is dropped\n# Soil_Type8 has minimal occurrence (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186) and no meaningful contribution to predictions.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Step 2: Soil_Type36\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type36 is dropped\n# Soil_Type36 shows near-zero variance (Mean = 0.0001, Std = 0.0103), making it irrelevant for predicting forest cover types.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Step 3: Soil_Type28\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type28 is dropped\n# Soil_Type28 has very low variability (Mean = 0.0016, Std = 0.0399) and negligible impact on the target variable.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type28'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Summary:\n1. Added Features:\n   - Elevation_To_Hydrology_3D_Ratio: Captures terrain elevation relative to 3D hydrology proximity.\n   - Overall_Sunlight_Intensity: Measures cumulative sunlight exposure during daylight hours.\n   - Road_Fire_Proximity_Factor: Highlights the interplay between road accessibility and wildfire-prone areas.\n\n2. Dropped Columns:\n   - Soil_Type8, Soil_Type36, Soil_Type28: Removed due to their negligible variability and predictive value.\n\nThis solution remains simple, intuitive, and ecologically relevant for improving downstream model predictions."), with improvements -
