opping redundant columns:\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Elevation_To_Slope_Ratio\npython\n# Elevation_To_Slope_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Elevation to Slope. This combines elevation and terrain steepness to understand environmental conditions affecting forest cover.\n# Usefulness: Steep slopes at varying elevations influence water runoff, soil erosion, and vegetation patterns—all critical factors for predicting forest cover types.\ndf['Elevation_To_Slope_Ratio'] = df['Elevation'] / (df['Slope'] + 1e-6)  # Adding a small value to avoid division by zero\n\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Combined_Sunlight_Avg\npython\n# Combined_Sunlight_Avg\n# Feature description: Compute a simple average of Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, and Hillshade_3pm to capture daylight exposure throughout the day.\n# Usefulness: Sunlight impacts vegetation growth and microclimates. A daytime average simplifies sunlight variability while retaining its predictive value for forest types.\ndf['Combined_Sunlight_Avg'] = (df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_Noon'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']) / 3\n\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Horizontal_Distance_Road_Fire_Ratio\npython\n# Horizontal_Distance_Road_Fire_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways to Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points. This measures the relative influence of roads and wildfire ignition proximity.\n# Usefulness: Accessibility (proximity to roads) and fire exposure (proximity to fire points) are important factors influencing vegetation and forest cover. This ratio captures their relationship intuitively.\ndf['Horizontal_Distance_Road_Fire_Ratio'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'] + 1e-6)  # Adding a small constant for division safety\n\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Irrelevant Columns:\n#### Reasoning: Soil type columns with near-zero variance and minimal occurrence offer little predictive value. Removing them streamlines the dataset and reduces 