2026-05-13 14:18:25,461 - INFO - Selected state: ("python\n# Step 1: Elevation_Diff_Hydro\n# Calculate the difference between Elevation and Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology\n# Feature description: This captures the vertical relationship between the elevation of the cell and the height of the nearest water body. It indicates whether the cell is at a higher or lower elevation relative to the water source, which could affect forest growth.\n# Usefulness: Certain tree types might prefer being at higher elevations above water bodies (well-drained soil) or lower elevations close to water sources.\n\ndf['Elevation_Diff_Hydro'] = df['Elevation'] - df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']\nend\n\npython\n# Step 2: Aspect_Sin and Aspect_Cos\n# Transform the Aspect feature using sine and cosine to account for its circular nature.\n# Feature description: Aspect (direction the slope faces) is circular, where 360° and 0° are essentially the same direction. Transforming it into its components allows the model to correctly learn patterns in directionality.\n# Usefulness: By representing direction in terms of sine and cosine components, we can capture patterns tied to the sun's position, wind exposure, and snow accumulation that might influence forest cover type.\n\nimport numpy as np\ndf['Aspect_Sin'] = np.sin(np.radians(df['Aspect']))\ndf['Aspect_Cos'] = np.cos(np.radians(df['Aspect']))\nend\n\npython\n# Step 3: Hydrology_Horizontal_Ratio\n# Calculate the ratio between Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology and Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways\n# Feature description: This measures the balance and competition between proximity to water and proximity to roads, both of which may influence forest distribution due to environmental gradients or human activity.\n# Usefulness: This provides insight into how environmental vs. anthropogenic factors influence the prediction, as certain forest types may dominate areas closer to water or roads.\n\ndf['Hydrology_Horizontal_Ratio'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] + 1e-6)  # Add small constant to avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type1' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type1' contains no variation (all zero values), thus not providing any predictive value to the model. Columns like these will only add noise.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type1'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type37' is dropped\n# Reason: Similar to 'Soil_Type1', 'Soil_Type37' contains no variation (all zero values). These columns lack information and will not improve the model's performance.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type37'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type15' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type15' also contains no variation (all zero values). Such columns should be dropped to maintain an efficient and clean dataset.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type15'], inplace=True)\nend", "python\n# Step 1: Hillshade_Mean\n# Feature description: Calculate the mean of the three Hillshade features (Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, Hillshade_3pm)\n# Usefulness: This provides a single summary feature representing the general amount of sunlight received throughout the day. Certain forest cover types may thrive under specific lighting conditions.\n\ndf['Hillshade_Mean'] = (df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_Noon'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']) / 3\nend\n\npython\n# Step 2: Slope_To_Hydrology\n# Feature description: Calculate the tangent of the slope between the point and the nearest water body using Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology and Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures the terrain's steepness relative to water, which might indicate how water drains or collects in the area, influencing forest type.\n\ndf['Slope_To_Hydrology'] = np.arctan(df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] + 1e-6))\nend\n\npython\n# Step 3: Road_Fire_Dist_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways to Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures the relationship between human activity (roads) and wildfire proximity, which might provide insights into forest cover based on fire susceptibility and human influence.\n\ndf['Road_Fire_Dist_Ratio'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type5' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type5' contains no variation (all zero values). This feature provides no useful information to the model and increases computation cost.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type5'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type14' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type14' also contains no variation (all zero values). Such features do not contribute to model performance and should be removed.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type14'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type37' is dropped\n# Reason: Similar to 'Soil_Type5' and 'Soil_Type14', 'Soil_Type37' contains no variation (all zero values). Removing such features reduces noise in the dataset.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type37'], inplace=True)\nend", "python\n# Step 1: Elevation_Normalized_To_Roadways\n# Feature description: Normalize Elevation by dividing it by Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways. This measures elevation relative to human infrastructure proximity, providing insight into elevation preferences for forest cover near roads.\n# Usefulness: Forest cover type might vary based on elevation in areas closer or farther from roads, as infrastructure affects water drainage, soil quality, and sunlight exposure.\n\ndf['Elevation_Normalized_To_Roadways'] = df['Elevation'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\npython\n# Step 2: Hydrology_Fire_Distance_Diff\n# Feature description: Compute the absolute difference between Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology and Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points. This captures how proximity to water and fire ignition sources differ spatially.\n# Usefulness: Forest cover type might be influenced by areas closer to water for moisture or farther from fire points for reduced fire risk.\n\ndf['Hydrology_Fire_Distance_Diff'] = abs(df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] - df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'])\nend\n\npython\n# Step 3: Soil_Wilderness_Interaction\n# Feature description: Aggregate the sum of all wilderness area indicators and soil type indicators into one feature. This represents a combined relationship between wilderness and soil properties.\n# Usefulness: Different soil types and wilderness areas have distinct ecological characteristics that influence forest cover type.\n\ndf['Soil_Wilderness_Interaction'] = df[['Wilderness_Area1', 'Wilderness_Area2', 'Wilderness_Area3', 'Wilderness_Area4']].sum(axis=1) + df[[col for col in df.columns if 'Soil_Type' in col]].sum(axis=1)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type1' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type1' contains no variation (all zero values). Removing it reduces computation without loss of predictive power.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type1'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type37' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type37' contains no variation (all zero values). Keeping it adds no predictive value and increases dataset noise.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type37'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type15' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type15' contains no variation (all zero values), offering no information for predicting forest cover type. Dropping ensures cleaner data.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type15'], inplace=True)\nend", "python\n# Feature: Elevation_To_Hydrology_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Elevation to Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology. This provides a measure of relative elevation compared to water proximity, capturing hydrological and elevation gradients.\n# Usefulness: Forest cover may depend on elevation relative to proximity to water sources, influencing both soil moisture and vegetation type.\ndf['Elevation_To_Hydrology_Ratio'] = df['Elevation'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] + 1e-6)  # Added small constant to prevent division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: Hillshade_Intensity_Range\n# Feature description: Compute the range (difference between max and min) of Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, and Hillshade_3pm. This captures variability in sunlight exposure throughout the day.\n# Usefulness: Certain forest cover types may thrive in areas with stable or highly variable sunlight, making this range a useful metric.\ndf['Hillshade_Intensity_Range'] = df[['Hillshade_9am', 'Hillshade_Noon', 'Hillshade_3pm']].max(axis=1) - df[['Hillshade_9am', 'Hillshade_Noon', 'Hillshade_3pm']].min(axis=1)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: Proximity_Factor_Avg\n# Feature description: Calculate the average of Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways, Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology, and Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points to summarize proximity to anthropogenic and environmental influences.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures combined proximity factors that might impact forest types, balancing environmental and human factors into a single metric.\ndf['Proximity_Factor_Avg'] = (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] + df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] + df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points']) / 3\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type8' is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type8 has minimal variance and mean occurrence (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186), providing no meaningful predictive value for forest cover type.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type36' is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type36 has negligible occurrence (Mean = 0.0001, Std = 0.0103) and lacks variability, making it irrelevant for model training or predictions.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type25' is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type25 shows low variability (Mean = 0.0008, Std = 0.0287) and negligible impact on predictions of forest cover type.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type25'], inplace=True)\nend", "### Possible next steps for feature engineering:\n\n#### Step 1: Elevation_To_Hydrology_3D_Ratio\npython\n# Elevation_To_Hydrology_3D_Ratio\n# Feature description: Compute the ratio of Elevation to the Euclidean distance to the nearest hydrology feature (combining both horizontal and vertical distances).\n# Usefulness: This feature highlights how elevation interacts with combined proximity to water sources, which are critical for soil moisture, terrain attributes, and vegetation growth patterns. Forest types may vary depending on elevation and distance from hydrology features.\ndf['Elevation_To_Hydrology_3D_Ratio'] = df['Elevation'] / (np.sqrt(df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology']2 + df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']2) + 1e-6)\nend\n\n---\n\n#### Step 2: Combined_Hillshade_Average\npython\n# Combined_Hillshade_Average\n# Feature description: Calculate the mean of Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, and Hillshade_3pm to summarize overall sunlight exposure throughout the day.\n# Usefulness: Simplifying sunlight exposure as a daily average helps capture the general light availability for forest cover types, without relying on individual time-specific values. Certain trees thrive under particular light conditions, and this measure provides a direct way to model that dependence.\ndf['Combined_Hillshade_Average'] = (df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_Noon'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']) / 3\nend\n\n---\n\n#### Step 3: Proximity_Mean_Ratio\npython\n# Proximity_Mean_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Proximity_Factor_Avg to Elevation. This combines proximity factors to water, fire points, and roadways, scaled by elevation to account for vertical terrain variations.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures the relationship between environmental proximity and elevation, providing insight into forest dynamics based on human and natural influences at varying altitudes. It simplifies interpretation while accounting for the dataset's spatial and elevation characteristics.\ndf['Proximity_Mean_Ratio'] = df['Proximity_Factor_Avg'] / (df['Elevation'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping redundant columns:\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type36\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type36 is dropped\n# Soil_Type36 has a near-zero mean (Mean = 0.0001) and very low variance (Std = 0.0103), contributing negligible predictive value for forest cover type.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type8\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type8 is dropped\n# Soil_Type8 has minimal variance (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186) and occurs in very few instances across the dataset, making it irrelevant for model predictions.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type28\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type28 is dropped\n# Soil_Type28 has very low variability (Mean = 0.0016, Std = 0.0399), and its lack of occurrence limits its usefulness in predicting forest cover types, so it is removed for efficiency.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type28'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Summary:\n1. Added Features:\n   - Elevation_To_Hydrology_3D_Ratio: Incorporates elevation and proximity to water sources in 3D space for better terrain representation.\n   - Combined_Hillshade_Average: Simplifies sunlight exposure throughout the day to model light patterns impacting forest dynamics.\n   - Proximity_Mean_Ratio: Represents proximity factors relative to elevation, balancing anthropogenic and natural influences across terrain types.\n\n2. Dropped Columns:\n   - Soil_Type36, Soil_Type8, Soil_Type28: Removed due to negligible variance, occurrence, and predictive value—streamlining the dataset for better performance and interpretability."), with improvements -
