rea2', 'Wilderness_Area3', 'Wilderness_Area4']].sum(axis=1) + df[[col for col in df.columns if 'Soil_Type' in col]].sum(axis=1)\nend\n\n### Dropping Redundant Columns: Low-Variance Soil Types\npython\n# Explanation why the column sets XX are dropped\n# Many soil type columns have near-zero mean values and insignificant variability (e.g., Soil_Type8, Soil_Type36), making them unlikely to contribute meaningful information to the model.\ncolumns_to_drop = ['Soil_Type8', 'Soil_Type28', 'Soil_Type34', 'Soil_Type36', 'Soil_Type25', 'Soil_Type37']\ndf.drop(columns=columns_to_drop, inplace=True)\nend\n\nEach new feature is designed to add interpretability while retaining simplicity, and redundant columns are removed based on their lack of variability and potential contribution. This approach ensures the dataset remains efficient and focused for downstream modeling.", "python\n# Feature: Elevation_To_Slope_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Elevation to Slope. This feature combines elevation and slope, which could influence soil drainage and forest cover type.\n# Usefulness: Steeper slopes combined with varying elevations can lead to different soil erosion patterns, impacting the growth and survival of specific forest types.\ndf['Elevation_To_Slope_Ratio'] = df['Elevation'] / (df['Slope'] + 1e-6)  # Adding small constant to avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: Combined_Sunlight_Exposure\n# Feature description: Compute a mean of Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, and Hillshade_3pm. This captures average sunlight exposure across the entire day.\n# Usefulness: Sunlight exposure influences vegetation and microclimates critical to forest cover type, as certain trees may thrive in areas with specific light patterns.\ndf['Combined_Sunlight_Exposure'] = (df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_Noon'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']) / 3\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: Proximity_Interaction\n# Feature description: Calculate the product of Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways, Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology, and Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points. This captures interaction effects of proximity to infrastructure, water, and fire points.\n# Usefulness: Interactions between human infrastructure, water sources, and fire proximity can significantly influence forest cover, as these factors govern both environmental and anthropogenic impacts.\ndf['Proximity_Interaction'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] * df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] * df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points']\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type28 is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type28 has near-zero mean and variance (Mean = 0.0016, Std = 0.0399), contributing little or no predictive power to the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type28'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type8 is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type8 has minimal occurrence (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186) and lacks variability, making it ineffective for predicting forest cover types.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type36 is dropped\n# Reason: Similar to other low-variance soil types, Soil_Type36 has an extremely small mean (Mean = 0.0001, Std = 0.0103) and is unlikely to provide meaningful information for predictions.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend", "python\n# Feature: Elevation_To_Hydrology_Ratio\n# Feature description: Compute the ratio of Elevation to the absolute value of Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology. This evaluates the relative vertical positioning of a point above surface water.\n# Usefulness: Captures hydrological influence on vegetation by linking elevation heights to proximity to water sources, which can impact soil moisture and forest cover type.\ndf['Elevation_To_Hydrology_Ratio'] = df['Elevation'] / (abs(df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']) + 1e-6)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: Combined_Hillshade_Index\n# Feature description: Calculate the weighted average of Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, and Hillshade_3pm to summarize overall sunlight exposure.\n# Usefulness: Sunlight exposure affects vegetation growth patterns and forest composition. This feature combines sunlight exposure across the day for a holistic measure.\ndf['Combined_Hillshade_Index'] = (0.3 * df['Hillshade_9am'] + 0.4 * df['Hillshade_Noon'] + 0.3 * df['Hillshade_3pm'])\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: Proximity_Factor\n# Feature description: Compute the geometric mean of Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways, Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points, and Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology.\n# Usefulness: This captures a combined factor of proximity to roads, fire points, and hydrology, which could influence forest cover type by representing human and environmental impact.\ndf['Proximity_Factor'] = (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] * \n                          df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'] * \n                          df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'])  (1/3)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type8 is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type8 has minimal variability (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186) and negligible occurrence, contributing no predictive value.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type36 is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type36 shows near-zero occurrence (Mean = 0.0001, Std = 0.0103), thus offering no useful information for predictions.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type28 is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type28 has very low variability (Mean = 0.0016, Std = 0.0399) and is unlikely to influence predictions of forest cover type significantly.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type28'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nEach of these steps thoughtfully enhances feature representation or removes irrelevant columns, ensuring the dataset remains concise and informative for modeling forest cover types.", "python\n# Feature: Vertical_Above_Hydrology\n# Feature description: Calculate the absolute vertical distance of a point above or below hydrology.\n# Usefulness: Differentiates points that are above or below the nearest water body, which could influence forest type due to soil drainage or water availability.\ndf['Vertical_Above_Hydrology'] = abs(df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology'])\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: Distance_Factor\n# Feature description: Calculate the geometric mean of Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology and Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways.\n# Usefulness: Combines environmental (water proximity) and anthropogenic (road proximity) influences in a single metric, which may impact forest coverage patterns more effectively than standalone features.\ndf['Distance_Factor'] = (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] * df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'])  (1/2)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: Hillshade_Skew\n# Feature description: Calculate the difference between Hillshade_Noon and the average of Hillshade_9am and Hillshade_3pm to capture the midday sunlight's deviation.\n# Usefulness: This skew metric captures light conditions during the most intense sunlight hours, potentially identifying areas with environmental conditions favoring certain forest types.\ndf['Hillshade_Skew'] = df['Hillshade_Noon'] - ((df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']) / 2)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type8' is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type8 has minimal occurrence (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186), offering negligible discriminative power.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type36' is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type36 shows near-zero mean (Mean = 0.0001, Std = 0.0103) and no variability, making it irrelevant for predictions.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type25' is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type25 has extremely low variability (Mean = 0.0008, Std = 0.0287) and negligible impact on predicting forest cover types.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type25'], inplace=True)\nend", "Here are three possible feature engineering steps to improve forest cover type predictions. These are designed to enhance features that provide meaningful ecological or anthropogenic insights based on the provided attributes:\n\npython\n# Elevation_To_Slope_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Elevation to Slope. This feature combines elevation and slope to evaluate the steepness of the terrain relative to its height.\n# Usefulness: Steeper slopes at varying elevations can affect water runoff, soil stability, and nutrient distribution—all of which influence growth patterns and forest cover type.\ndf['Elevation_To_Slope_Ratio'] = df['Elevation'] / (df['Slope'] + 1e-6)  # Adding small constant to avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Overall_Sunlight_Exposure\n# Feature description: Compute the mean of Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, and Hillshade_3pm. This captures the average sunlight exposure across the day.\n# Usefulness: Different forest cover types thrive under specific light conditions. Summarizing daily sunlight exposure provides an intuitive and simple representation of light's effect on vegetation.\ndf['Overall_Sunlight_Exposure'] = (df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_Noon'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']) / 3\nend\n\npython\n# Hydrology_Road_Fire_Interaction\n# Feature description: Calculate the product of Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology, Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways, and Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points. This captures interaction effects of environmental and anthropogenic proximity.\n# Usefulness: Forest cover is influenced by the combined proximity of water, infrastructure, and wildfire susceptibility. This interaction feature can help model these complex relationships.\ndf['Hydrology_Road_Fire_Interaction'] = (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] * \n                                         df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] * \n                                         df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'])\nend\n\nColumns to drop based on low variance or lack of contribution to the target:\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type8' is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type8 has minimal variance (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186), making it unlikely to contribute meaningfully to forest cover predictions.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type36' is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type36 shows near-zero variability (Mean = 0.0001, Std = 0.0103), offering negligible predictive power.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type28' is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type28 has very low variance (Mean = 0.0016, Std = 0.0399), making it unlikely to influence model predictions significantly.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type28'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nEach added feature and dropped column is designed to be simple, direct, and interpretable while leveraging relationships within the dataset to provide enhanced predictive power for the forest cover type.", "Here are three simple, direct, and intuitive feature engineering steps designed to improve the downstream predictive model, along with explanations for dropping certain columns:\n\n### Feature Engineering Steps:\n\npython\n# Elevation_To_Slope_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Elevation to Slope. This feature combines elevation and terrain steepness to better understand water runoff, soil stability, and vegetation growth patterns.\n# Usefulness: Steep slopes at varying elevations affect environmental conditions such as soil erosion and water availability, which strongly influence forest cover types.\ndf['Elevation_To_Slope_Ratio'] = df['Elevation'] / (df['Slope'] + 1e-6)  # Adding small constant to avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Overall_Hillshade_Exposure\n# Feature description: Compute the mean of Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, and Hillshade_3pm to capture average sunlight exposure throughout the day.\n# Usefulness: Forest types differ in their ability to thrive under varying light conditions. A daily average of sunlight exposure simplifies these effects while retaining predictive power.\ndf['Overall_Hillshade_Exposure'] = (df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_Noon'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']) / 3\nend\n\npython\n# Hydrology_Road_Proximity_Interaction\n# Feature description: Calculate the interaction effect between Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways and Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology using their product.\n# Usefulness: Proximity to water and roads might interact to influence forest types due to differences in human activity and environmental gradients. This feature captures both factors together.\ndf['Hydrology_Road_Proximity_Interaction'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] * df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology']\nend\n\n### Dropping Redundant Columns:\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type8' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type8' has minimal variance (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186), contributing negligible predictive power to the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type36' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type36' contains near-zero mean and variance (Mean = 0.0001, Std = 0.0103), making it ineffective for predicting forest cover types.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type28' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type28' has low variability (Mean = 0.0016, Std = 0.0399) and is unlikely to contribute meaningful information to model predictions.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type28'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nEach added feature is designed to capture meaningful interactions or relationships within the dataset, while the dropped columns help streamline the dataset by removing attributes with negligible impact.", "Here are three simple, direct, and effective feature engineering steps, along with explanations for dropping redundant columns:\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Elevation_To_Slope_Ratio\npython\n# Elevation_To_Slope_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Elevation to Slope. This combines elevation and terrain steepness to understand environmental conditions affecting forest cover.\n# Usefulness: Steep slopes at varying elevations influence water runoff, soil erosion, and vegetation patterns—all critical factors for predicting forest cover types.\ndf['Elevation_To_Slope_Ratio'] = df['Elevation'] / (df['Slope'] + 1e-6)  # Adding a small value to avoid division by zero\n\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Combined_Sunlight_Avg\npython\n# Combined_Sunlight_Avg\n# Feature description: Compute a simple average of Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, and Hillshade_3pm to capture daylight exposure throughout the day.\n# Usefulness: Sunlight impacts vegetation growth and microclimates. A daytime average simplifies sunlight variability while retaining its predictive value for forest types.\ndf['Combined_Sunlight_Avg'] = (df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_Noon'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']) / 3\n\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Horizontal_Distance_Road_Fire_Ratio\npython\n# Horizontal_Distance_Road_Fire_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways to Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points. This measures the relative influence of roads and wildfire ignition proximity.\n# Usefulness: Accessibility (proximity to roads) and fire exposure (proximity to fire points) are important factors influencing vegetation and forest cover. This ratio captures their relationship intuitively.\ndf['Horizontal_Distance_Road_Fire_Ratio'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'] + 1e-6)  # Adding a small constant for division safety\n\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Irrelevant Columns:\n#### Reasoning: Soil type columns with near-zero variance and minimal occurrence offer little predictive value. Removing them streamlines the dataset and reduces redundancy.\n\n---\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type8' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type8' has minimal variance (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186) and negligible occurrence across the dataset.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\n\nend\n\n---\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type36' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type36' shows near-zero mean (Mean = 0.0001, Std = 0.0103) and no variability, making it ineffective for predictions.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\n\nend\n\n---\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type28' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type28' has low variability (Mean = 0.0016, Std = 0.0399) and is unlikely to contribute significantly to predicting forest cover types.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type28'], inplace=True)\n\nend\n\n---\n\nThis approach focuses on creating meaningful and intuitive features while simplifying the dataset by removing low-variance columns. Each step is designed to improve predictive model performance without unnecessary complexity.", "Here are three possible next steps for feature engineering and optimizations designed to improve predictions for forest cover type (1 or 2):\n\n### Step 1: Elevation_To_Hydrology_Ratio\npython\n# Elevation_To_Hydrology_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Elevation to Vertical Distance To Hydrology, representing the relative vertical positioning of a cell above or below nearby water features.\n# Usefulness: This captures the relationship between terrain elevation and proximity to water sources, which significantly impacts vegetation types and forest cover due to factors like water availability and soil moisture.\ndf['Elevation_To_Hydrology_Ratio'] = df['Elevation'] / (abs(df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']) + 1e-6)  # Add small value for safety.\nend\n\n### Step 2: Combined_Sunlight_Avg\npython\n# Combined_Sunlight_Avg\n# Feature description: Compute the average of Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, and Hillshade_3pm to represent overall sunlight exposure across the day.\n# Usefulness: Forest growth and vegetation vary based on the amount of sunlight an area receives over the day. This feature simplifies the representation of light intensity while retaining its overall impact.\ndf['Combined_Sunlight_Avg'] = (df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_Noon'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']) / 3\nend\n\n### Step 3: Hydrology_Distance_Slope_Ratio\npython\n# Hydrology_Distance_Slope_Ratio\n# Feature description: Compute the ratio of Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology to Slope, gauging closeness to water sources relative to terrain steepness.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures the interplay between slope and water proximity, which influences water retention, erosion, and vegetation distribution in the forest.\ndf['Hydrology_Distance_Slope_Ratio'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] / (df['Slope'] + 1e-6)  # Add small value for safety.\nend\n\n### Dropping Redundant Columns\n#### Column: Soil_Type3\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type3 is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type3 has minimal variance with a very low mean and standard deviation, contributing negligible predictive value.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type3'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type25\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type25 is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type25 shows near-zero variance and negligible occurrence, making it uninformative for the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type25'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type17\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type17 is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type17 has very low variability, providing limited value to improve the predictive power of the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type17'], inplace=True)\nend  \n\nThese steps aim to generate meaningful transformations to better capture the relationships between features and their ecological and spatial significance while removing uninformative features to streamline model training. Combined, these enhancements aim to improve prediction performance.", "Here are three simple, direct, and intuitive feature engineering steps to improve predictions for forest cover type (1 or 2), along with explanations for dropping redundant columns:\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Distance_To_Hydrology_Combined\npython\n# Distance_To_Hydrology_Combined\n# Feature description: Computes the Euclidean distance to the nearest hydrology feature using both horizontal and vertical distances.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures combined proximity to surface water sources, which impacts soil moisture, vegetation growth, and forest cover types. A single unified metric is more interpretable and effective than separate horizontal and vertical distances.\ndf['Distance_To_Hydrology_Combined'] = (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology']2 + df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']2)0.5\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Elevation_To_Slope_Ratio\npython\n# Elevation_To_Slope_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculates the ratio of Elevation to Slope to capture terrain ruggedness.\n# Usefulness: This feature combines elevation and steepness to highlight terrain variability. Rugged terrain often correlates with distinct water flow, erosion, and vegetation patterns—important factors influencing forest cover types.\ndf['Elevation_To_Slope_Ratio'] = df['Elevation'] / (df['Slope'] + 1e-6)  # Adding small constant to prevent division by zero\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Combined_Hillshade_Average\npython\n# Combined_Hillshade_Average\n# Feature description: Calculates the average of Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, and Hillshade_3pm to capture overall sunlight exposure throughout the day.\n# Usefulness: Forest types are influenced by daily sunlight exposure. This feature simplifies the representation of light intensity while retaining its predictive power for vegetation growth and microclimate conditions.\ndf['Combined_Hillshade_Average'] = (df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_Noon'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']) / 3\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Redundant Columns:\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type36\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type36 is dropped\n# Soil_Type36 has a near-zero mean (Mean = 0.0001, Std = 0.0103) and negligible variance, making it irrelevant for predicting forest cover types.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type28\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type28 is dropped\n# Soil_Type28 has very low variability (Mean = 0.0016, Std = 0.0399) and minimal impact on predicting the target variable.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type28'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type8\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type8 is dropped\n# Soil_Type8 has minimal occurrence (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186), contributing negligible predictive value to the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Summary:\n1. Added Features:\n   - Distance_To_Hydrology_Combined: Unified representation of hydrology distances.\n   - Elevation_To_Slope_Ratio: Terrain ruggedness, combining elevation and slope.\n   - Combined_Hillshade_Average: Simplified sunlight exposure throughout the day.\n\n2. Dropped Columns:\n   - Removed low-variance soil types (Soil_Type36, Soil_Type28, Soil_Type8) to simplify the dataset and focus on informative features.\n\nThis approach is simple, direct, and leverages existing features to generate meaningful interactions and relationships while streamlining the dataset.", "python\n# Combined_Hillshade_Mean\n# Feature description: Calculate the mean of Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, and Hillshade_3pm to represent overall daily sunlight exposure.\n# Usefulness: Sunlight exposure influences vegetation and microclimate, affecting forest cover type. A simple average captures overall light intensity, reducing feature sparsity.\ndf['Combined_Hillshade_Mean'] = (df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_Noon'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']) / 3\nend\n\npython\n# Hydrology_Distance_3D\n# Feature description: Compute the Euclidean distance combining Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology and Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology.\n# Usefulness: A single feature representing the true proximity to surface water captures both horizontal and vertical hydrology effects, which influence soil moisture and vegetation distribution.\ndf['Hydrology_Distance_3D'] = np.sqrt(df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology']2 + df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']2)\nend\n\npython\n# Road_Hydrology_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways to Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology.\n# Usefulness: This feature examines the relative proximity of a point to roads versus water sources, capturing potential trade-offs between human activity (roads) and natural hydrological influence.\ndf['Road_Hydrology_Ratio'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type36 is dropped\n# Soil_Type36 has a near-zero mean (Mean = 0.0001) and variance (Std = 0.0103), making it irrelevant for predicting forest cover types.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type8 is dropped\n# Soil_Type8 has minimal occurrence (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186), contributing negligible predictive value to the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type28 is dropped\n# Soil_Type28 has low variability (Mean = 0.0016, Std = 0.0399) and minimal impact on the target variable, leading to limited utility.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type28'], inplace=True)\nend", "python\n# Elevation_To_Hydrology_Ratio  \n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Elevation to the absolute value of Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology. \n# Usefulness: This evaluates the relative height of a cell compared to the nearest water source, which impacts soil moisture and vegetation. Higher ratios can signify terrain characteristics with distinct vegetation and soil profiles.  \ndf['Elevation_To_Hydrology_Ratio'] = df['Elevation'] / (abs(df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']) + 1e-6)  # Adding small constant to avoid division by zero  \nend  \n\npython\n# Combined_Hillshade_Average  \n# Feature description: Calculate the average of Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, and Hillshade_3pm to represent overall sunlight exposure throughout the day.  \n# Usefulness: Sunlight exposure influences vegetation growth and forest cover due to factors like photosynthesis and microclimate. Averaging these values provides a simplified yet comprehensive view of light's impact throughout the day.  \ndf['Combined_Hillshade_Average'] = (df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_Noon'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']) / 3  \nend  \n\npython\n# Distance_To_Hydrology_Combined  \n# Feature description: Compute the Euclidean distance to the nearest hydrology feature using Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology and Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology.  \n# Usefulness: This unified metric captures both horizontal and vertical proximities to water features, directly impacting soil moisture and vegetation patterns in the forest.  \ndf['Distance_To_Hydrology_Combined'] = np.sqrt((df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology']2) + (df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']2))  \nend  \n\n### Dropping Redundant Columns:  \n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type28 is dropped  \n# Soil_Type28 has minimal variance (Mean = 0.0016, Std = 0.0399) and negligible predictive power for the model.  \ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type28'], inplace=True)  \nend  \n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type8 is dropped  \n# Soil_Type8 contains near-zero mean (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186), indicating minimal variability and contribution to predictions.  \ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)  \nend  \n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type36 is dropped  \n# Soil_Type36 has an extremely low mean (Mean = 0.0001, Std = 0.0103) and variance, making it irrelevant for predicting forest cover types.  \ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)  \nend  \n\n### Summary:  \n1. Added Features:  \n   - Elevation_To_Hydrology_Ratio: Evaluates terrain height relative to hydrology features.  \n   - Combined_Hillshade_Average: Simplifies sunlight exposure throughout the day.  \n   - Distance_To_Hydrology_Combined: Unified distance metric for proximity to water sources.  \n\n2. Dropped Columns:  \n   - Soil_Type28, Soil_Type8, Soil_Type36: Removed due to negligible variability and predictive value.  \n\nThis approach remains simple, direct, interpretable, and aligns with the dataset's ecological and terrain-based context.", "Here are three simple, direct, and intuitive feature engineering steps that aim to improve predictions for forest cover type (1 or 2), along with explanations for dropping redundant columns. The approach is designed to avoid prior mistakes while focusing on meaningful and interpretable features.\n\n### Step 1: Elevation_Hydrology_Diff_Ratio\npython\n# Elevation_Hydrology_Diff_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of the vertical distance to hydrology to the difference between elevation and vertical distance to hydrology.\n# Usefulness: This feature reflects the relative positioning of terrain elevation compared to nearby water sources. It highlights terrain moisture availability and microclimates affecting forest types.\ndf['Elevation_Hydrology_Diff_Ratio'] = abs(df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']) / (df['Elevation_diff_Hydrology'] + 1e-6)  # Add small value to avoid division by zero\nend\n\n### Step 2: Road_Hydrology_Proximity_Ratio\npython\n# Road_Hydrology_Proximity_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways to Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology.\n# Usefulness: This captures the trade-off between proximity to human infrastructure (roads) and natural water sources. Areas closer to one versus the other may indicate different forest management or natural conditions.\ndf['Road_Hydrology_Proximity_Ratio'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] + 1e-6)  # Add small value to avoid division by zero\nend\n\n### Step 3: Total_Hillshade_Variation\npython\n# Total_Hillshade_Variation\n# Feature description: Calculate the total variation in sunlight exposure as the sum of absolute differences between Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, and Hillshade_3pm.\n# Usefulness: This feature provides a measure of sunlight variability throughout the day, which influences vegetation patterns and forest types adapted to different light conditions.\ndf['Total_Hillshade_Variation'] = abs(df['Hillshade_9am'] - df['Hillshade_Noon']) + abs(df['Hillshade_Noon'] - df['Hillshade_3pm']) + abs(df['Hillshade_9am'] - df['Hillshade_3pm'])\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Redundant Columns:\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type8\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type8 is dropped\n# Soil_Type8 has minimal variance (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186) and occurs in very few instances in the dataset, contributing negligible predictive power.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type36\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type36 is dropped\n# Soil_Type36 shows near-zero mean (Mean = 0.0001, Std = 0.0103) and negligible variability, making it irrelevant for predicting forest cover types.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type28\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type28 is dropped\n# Soil_Type28 has very low variability (Mean = 0.0016, Std = 0.0399) and limited predictive value for forest cover types.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type28'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Summary:\n1. Added Features:\n   - Elevation_Hydrology_Diff_Ratio: Captures how vertical hydrology distance varies relative to elevation differences, highlighting terrain moisture conditions.\n   - Road_Hydrology_Proximity_Ratio: Reflects the trade-off between proximity to roads and water, suggesting natural versus human-influenced environments.\n   - Total_Hillshade_Variation: Measures sunlight variability, key for understanding vegetation response.\n\n2. Dropped Columns:\n   - Soil_Type8, Soil_Type36, Soil_Type28: Removed due to minimal variance and negligible predictive value.\n\nThis solution ensures meaningful feature transformations while simplifying the dataset to improve downstream model performance.", "Here are three simple, direct, and intuitive feature engineering steps for improving predictions of forest cover type, along with reasons for dropping redundant columns.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Elevation_To_Hydrology_Combined_Ratio\npython\n# Elevation_To_Hydrology_Combined_Ratio\n# Feature description: Compute the ratio of Elevation to the Euclidean distance to the nearest hydrology feature.\n# Usefulness: This combines elevation and proximity to water sources to quantify how elevation interacts with both horizontal and vertical distances to hydrology, which impacts soil moisture and vegetation growth.\ndf['Elevation_To_Hydrology_Combined_Ratio'] = df['Elevation'] / (np.sqrt(df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology']2 + df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']2) + 1e-6)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Hillshade_Diff_Noon_Mean\npython\n# Hillshade_Diff_Noon_Mean\n# Feature description: Calculate the difference between Hillshade_Noon and the average of Hillshade_9am and Hillshade_3pm to measure midday light intensity deviation.\n# Usefulness: Midday sunlight deviation helps identify regions with unique light conditions. Forest cover type is influenced by light availability, making this feature valuable for highlighting microclimatic differences.\ndf['Hillshade_Diff_Noon_Mean'] = df['Hillshade_Noon'] - (df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']) / 2\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Wilderness_Soil_Composition\npython\n# Wilderness_Soil_Composition\n# Feature description: Combine all Wilderness Area indicators and Soil Type indicators into a single feature by summing their values for each point.\n# Usefulness: This feature summarizes the ecological and soil diversity of a location, leveraging interactions between regional wilderness zones and soil variability to better capture environmental factors affecting forest cover types.\ndf['Wilderness_Soil_Composition'] = df[['Wilderness_Area1', 'Wilderness_Area2', 'Wilderness_Area3', 'Wilderness_Area4']].sum(axis=1) + df[[col for col in df.columns if 'Soil_Type' in col]].sum(axis=1)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Redundant Columns\n#### Column: Soil_Type28\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type28 is dropped\n# Soil_Type28 has minimal variance (Mean = 0.0016, Std = 0.0399), offering negligible predictive power.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type28'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type8\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type8 is dropped\n# Soil_Type8 has a near-zero mean (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186), contributing little or no variance to improve predictions.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type36\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type36 is dropped\n# Soil_Type36 shows near-zero occurrence (Mean = 0.0001, Std = 0.0103), making it irrelevant for the task.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Summary:\n1. Added Features:\n   - Elevation_To_Hydrology_Combined_Ratio: Combines elevation and hydrology distances to capture environmental interactions crucial for forest cover.\n   - Hillshade_Diff_Noon_Mean: Highlights midday light deviations for understanding vegetation-dependent microclimates.\n   - Wilderness_Soil_Composition: Aggregates wilderness and soil indicators into a single measure reflecting environmental diversity.\n\n2. Dropped Columns:\n   - Soil_Type28, Soil_Type8, Soil_Type36: Removed due to low variance and negligible predictive power.\n\nThis approach enhances the dataset by adding meaningful transformations while removing redundant features. It's simple, direct, and aligned with the ecological context of the task.", "Here are three possible next steps, executed simply, directly, and intuitively, along with reasons for dropping redundant columns:\n\n### Step 1: Vertical_Hydrology_Abs\npython\n# Vertical_Hydrology_Abs\n# Feature description: Compute the absolute value of Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology.\n# Usefulness: This accounts for the distance irrespective of above or below water level, emphasizing how far the cell is from the water source, which impacts soil moisture and vegetation patterns.\ndf['Vertical_Hydrology_Abs'] = abs(df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology'])\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Hydrology_Euclidean_Distance\npython\n# Hydrology_Euclidean_Distance\n# Feature description: Calculate the Euclidean distance to the nearest hydrology feature using Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology and Vertical_Hydrology_Abs.\n# Usefulness: Combining horizontal and vertical distances into a single metric reflects the proximity to water sources, crucial for understanding terrain moisture and vegetation dependence.\ndf['Hydrology_Euclidean_Distance'] = np.sqrt(df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology']2 + df['Vertical_Hydrology_Abs']2)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Road_Slope_Interaction\npython\n# Road_Slope_Interaction\n# Feature description: Calculate the product of Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways and Slope.\n# Usefulness: This feature combines the steepness of the terrain and proximity to roads, which could influence forest accessibility, erosion, and vegetation patterns depending on terrain ruggedness.\ndf['Road_Slope_Interaction'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] * df['Slope']\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Redundant Columns:\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type8\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type8 is dropped\n# Soil_Type8 has minimal variance (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186) and occurs in very few instances, contributing negligible predictive power.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type28\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type28 is dropped\n# Soil_Type28 has very low variance (Mean = 0.0016, Std = 0.0399) and does not provide meaningful information for forest cover prediction.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type28'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type36\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type36 is dropped\n# Soil_Type36 has a near-zero mean (Mean = 0.0001, Std = 0.0103) and no significant variability, making it irrelevant for predictions.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Summary:\n1. Added Features:\n   - Vertical_Hydrology_Abs: Highlights water proximity irrespective of elevation relationship, emphasizing terrain moisture impact.\n   - Hydrology_Euclidean_Distance: Combines horizontal and vertical water distance for a cohesive measure of hydrology effects.\n   - Road_Slope_Interaction: Captures interaction between steep terrain and proximity to roads, relevant for forest patterns.\n   \n2. Dropped Columns:\n   - Soil_Type8, Soil_Type28, Soil_Type36: Removed due to minimal variance and negligible effect on prediction quality.\n\nThis solution focuses on real-world interpretability and enhances the dataset without unnecessary complexity.", "python\n# Hydrology_Distance_3D\n# Feature description: Compute the Euclidean distance to the nearest hydrology feature, combining horizontal and vertical distances.\n# Usefulness: This captures true proximity to water, which influences soil moisture, drainage, and vegetation patterns, making it highly relevant for forest cover predictions.\ndf['Hydrology_Distance_3D'] = np.sqrt(df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology']2 + df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']2)\nend\n\n\npython\n# Elevation_Relative_To_Mean_Slope\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Elevation_Above_Mean to Slope in the area.\n# Usefulness: This combines elevation relative to the dataset's mean with slope, capturing terrain features that may impact water runoff, erosion, and vegetation growth, which are critical for identifying forest cover types.\ndf['Elevation_Relative_To_Mean_Slope'] = df['Elevation_Above_Mean'] / (df['Slope'] + 1e-6)  # Add small constant to prevent division by zero\nend\n\n\npython\n# Aspect_Interaction\n# Feature description: Combine Aspect_Cos and Aspect_Sin into a single metric to represent aspect interaction.\n# Usefulness: Aspect measures terrain directionality, which influences exposure to sunlight and wind. Capturing both components in a single interaction improves model understanding of these complex relationships.\ndf['Aspect_Interaction'] = df['Aspect_Cos'] * df['Aspect_Sin']\nend\n\n\n### Dropping Columns:\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type8 is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type36 is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type28 is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type28'], inplace=True)\nend", "python\n# Elevation_Hydrology_Relative\n# Feature description: Compute the ratio of Elevation to the sum of horizontal and vertical hydrology distances. This feature represents the relative prominence of elevation compared to proximity to water sources.\n# Usefulness: This feature effectively captures the terrain's elevation in conjunction with its proximity to water, which influences soil moisture and vegetation types.\ndf['Elevation_Hydrology_Relative'] = df['Elevation'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] + abs(df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']) + 1e-6)\nend\n\npython\n# Hydrology_3D_Euclidean\n# Feature description: Calculate the Euclidean distance to the nearest hydrology feature using both horizontal and vertical hydrology distances.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures the true proximity to water sources, combining horizontal and vertical dimensions, which are critical for understanding vegetation patterns and water availability.\ndf['Hydrology_3D_Euclidean'] = np.sqrt(df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology']  2 + df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']  2)\nend\n\npython\n# Aspect_Solar_Interaction\n# Feature description: Compute the product of Aspect_Cos and Aspect_Sin to represent interactions between terrain orientation components.\n# Usefulness: This feature combines directional orientation (cosine and sine of aspect), which influences sunlight exposure and wind patterns, and may differentiate forest growth based on microclimates.\ndf['Aspect_Solar_Interaction'] = df['Aspect_Cos'] * df['Aspect_Sin']\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type8 is dropped\n# Soil_Type8 has minimal variance (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186) and occurs in very few instances in the dataset, providing negligible predictive power.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type36 is dropped\n# Soil_Type36 shows a near-zero mean (Mean = 0.0001, Std = 0.0103) and negligible variability, making it irrelevant for predicting forest cover types.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type28 is dropped\n# Soil_Type28 has very low variability (Mean = 0.0016, Std = 0.0399) and minimal impact on the prediction target, offering limited predictive value.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type28'], inplace=True)\nend"]###,
