2026-05-13 14:38:21,513 - INFO - Selected state: ("python\n# Step 1: Elevation_Diff_Hydro\n# Calculate the difference between Elevation and Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology\n# Feature description: This captures the vertical relationship between the elevation of the cell and the height of the nearest water body. It indicates whether the cell is at a higher or lower elevation relative to the water source, which could affect forest growth.\n# Usefulness: Certain tree types might prefer being at higher elevations above water bodies (well-drained soil) or lower elevations close to water sources.\n\ndf['Elevation_Diff_Hydro'] = df['Elevation'] - df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']\nend\n\npython\n# Step 2: Aspect_Sin and Aspect_Cos\n# Transform the Aspect feature using sine and cosine to account for its circular nature.\n# Feature description: Aspect (direction the slope faces) is circular, where 360° and 0° are essentially the same direction. Transforming it into its components allows the model to correctly learn patterns in directionality.\n# Usefulness: By representing direction in terms of sine and cosine components, we can capture patterns tied to the sun's position, wind exposure, and snow accumulation that might influence forest cover type.\n\nimport numpy as np\ndf['Aspect_Sin'] = np.sin(np.radians(df['Aspect']))\ndf['Aspect_Cos'] = np.cos(np.radians(df['Aspect']))\nend\n\npython\n# Step 3: Hydrology_Horizontal_Ratio\n# Calculate the ratio between Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology and Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways\n# Feature description: This measures the balance and competition between proximity to water and proximity to roads, both of which may influence forest distribution due to environmental gradients or human activity.\n# Usefulness: This provides insight into how environmental vs. anthropogenic factors influence the prediction, as certain forest types may dominate areas closer to water or roads.\n\ndf['Hydrology_Horizontal_Ratio'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] + 1e-6)  # Add small constant to avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type1' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type1' contains no variation (all zero values), thus not providing any predictive value to the model. Columns like these will only add noise.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type1'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type37' is dropped\n# Reason: Similar to 'Soil_Type1', 'Soil_Type37' contains no variation (all zero values). These columns lack information and will not improve the model's performance.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type37'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type15' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type15' also contains no variation (all zero values). Such columns should be dropped to maintain an efficient and clean dataset.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type15'], inplace=True)\nend", "python\n# Step 1: Vertical_Horizontal_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio between Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology and Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology.\n# Usefulness: This feature combines vertical and horizontal distances, which could better capture terrain complexity and the relationship between elevation and water proximity. These factors might influence forest type distribution.\n\ndf['Vertical_Horizontal_Ratio'] = df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] + 1e-6)  # Adding small constant to avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Step 2: Road_Fire_Proximity_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio between Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways and Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points.\n# Usefulness: This feature quantifies proximity balance between roads and fire points. It may highlight areas with higher human activity due to roads, or areas closer to fire hazards, which can influence cover type survival and adaptation.\n\ndf['Road_Fire_Proximity_Ratio'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'] + 1e-6)  # Adding small constant to avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Step 3: Hydrology_All_Distance_Sum\n# Feature description: Calculate the sum of Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology and Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology.\n# Usefulness: This combines both dimensions of water proximity (horizontal and vertical) into a single feature, providing a more holistic measure of water influence on forest type.\n\ndf['Hydrology_All_Distance_Sum'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] + df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type14' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type14' contains no variation (all zero values), offering no predictive value and adding unnecessary noise to the dataset.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type14'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type5' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type5' also contains no variation (all zero values). Dropping this column keeps the dataset clean and efficient for modeling.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type5'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type37' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type37' contains no variation (all zero values) and, similar to other dropped columns, lacks predictive information for the model task.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type37'], inplace=True)\nend", "python\n# Elevation_Distance_To_Hydrology_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio between Elevation and the sum of Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology and Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology.\n# Usefulness: This captures the relative elevation of a cell compared to its overall proximity to hydrological features, which may influence forest growth patterns due to moisture availability and terrain.\ndf['Elevation_Distance_To_Hydrology_Ratio'] = df['Elevation'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] + df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\npython\n# Combined_Hillshade_Exposure\n# Feature description: Compute the average of Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, and Hillshade_3pm to represent overall sunlight exposure during the day.\n# Usefulness: Sunlight exposure plays a critical role in ecosystem dynamics and vegetation growth. Combining these into a single feature simplifies the model and captures its overall effect on forest cover.\ndf['Combined_Hillshade_Exposure'] = (df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_Noon'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']) / 3\nend\n\npython\n# Fire_Road_Proximity_Difference\n# Feature description: Calculate the difference between Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points and Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways.\n# Usefulness: The difference between proximity to fire ignition points and roads may highlight regions dominated by natural vs human influences, which can impact forest cover types.\ndf['Fire_Road_Proximity_Difference'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'] - df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways']\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type7' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type7' contains no variation (all zero values), providing no useful information for predictions.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type7'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type36' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type36' also contains no variation (all zero values). Columns with no variability should be removed to avoid redundancy and noise.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type15' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type15' contains no variation (all zero values). Removing such columns keeps the dataset streamlined for modeling.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type15'], inplace=True)\nend", "python\n# Vertical_Absolute_Distance_To_Hydrology\n# Feature description: Calculate the absolute vertical distance of a cell to its nearest hydrology feature.\n# Usefulness: This captures whether a cell is above or below water level in magnitude (ignoring sign), which is important for understanding soil moisture, drainage, and vegetation types.\ndf['Vertical_Absolute_Distance_To_Hydrology'] = abs(df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology'])\nend\n\npython\n# Slope_Elevation_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Slope to Elevation. This evaluates the steepness of the terrain relative to its height.\n# Usefulness: This helps assess terrain ruggedness, which impacts soil erosion, water retention, and forest cover changes due to ecological stress.\ndf['Slope_Elevation_Ratio'] = df['Slope'] / (df['Elevation'] + 1e-6)  # Adding small constant to avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Hydrology_Road_Combined_Proximity\n# Feature description: Calculate the sum of Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology and Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways to account for combined proximity to water and infrastructure.\n# Usefulness: Forest cover may be influenced by simultaneous proximity to natural (water) and human (roads) factors, which could affect accessibility and ecological balance.\ndf['Hydrology_Road_Combined_Proximity'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] + df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways']\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type15' is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type15 contains no variation (all zero values), offering no predictive value and adding unnecessary noise.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type15'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type36' is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type36 lacks variability (Mean = 0.0001, Std = 0.0103), providing no significant information for forest cover predictions.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type7' is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type7 contains only zeros, indicating no predictive information is provided by this feature. It adds redundancy to the dataset.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type7'], inplace=True)\nend", "Here are three simple, direct, and intuitive feature engineering steps designed to improve predictions for forest cover type, along with explanations for dropping redundant columns:\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Vertical_Horizontal_Combined_Euclidean  \npython\n# Vertical_Horizontal_Combined_Euclidean  \n# Feature description: Compute the Euclidean distance combining both vertical and horizontal distances to hydrology.  \n# Usefulness: This feature encapsulates proximity to water sources in a single value, accounting for combined effects of elevation and horizontal distance on terrain moisture and vegetation patterns.  \ndf['Vertical_Horizontal_Combined_Euclidean'] = np.sqrt(df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology']2 + df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']2)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Combined_Road_Fire_Hazard  \npython\n# Combined_Road_Fire_Hazard  \n# Feature description: Calculate the combined proximity to roads and fire ignition points by summing their horizontal distances.  \n# Usefulness: This feature represents the influence of both infrastructure and wildfire hazards on vegetation and forest cover, as areas closer to such features may have unique dynamics.  \ndf['Combined_Road_Fire_Hazard'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] + df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points']\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Elevation_Above_Hydrology  \npython\n# Elevation_Above_Hydrology  \n# Feature description: Compute the difference between elevation and the absolute vertical distance to hydrology.  \n# Usefulness: This helps identify how much higher or lower a cell is relative to nearby water sources, which may affect drainage, soil moisture, and vegetation types conducive to forest cover.  \ndf['Elevation_Above_Hydrology'] = df['Elevation'] - abs(df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology'])\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Redundant Columns:\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type8  \npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type8 is dropped  \n# Soil_Type8 has minimal variance (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186) and occurs in very few instances, contributing negligible predictive power.  \ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type36  \npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type36 is dropped  \n# Soil_Type36 has a near-zero mean (Mean = 0.0001, Std = 0.0103) and negligible variability, making it irrelevant for forest cover prediction.  \ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n#### Column: Soil_Type28  \npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type28 is dropped  \n# Soil_Type28 has very low variability (Mean = 0.0016, Std = 0.0399) and limited contribution to improving prediction accuracy.  \ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type28'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Summary:\n\n1. Features Added:\n   - Vertical_Horizontal_Combined_Euclidean: Captures true proximity to water sources.\n   - Combined_Road_Fire_Hazard: Reflects cumulative effect of roads and fire on forest vegetation.\n   - Elevation_Above_Hydrology: Highlights relative elevation compared to hydrology, useful for understanding terrain-driven vegetation patterns.\n\n2. Columns Dropped:\n   - Soil_Type8, Soil_Type36, Soil_Type28: Removed due to low variance and negligible predictive power.\n\nThis approach prioritizes meaningful features and streamlines the dataset by removing redundant columns, ensuring better interpretability and utility for modeling forest cover type."), with improvements -
