ate the ratio of Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways to Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures the relationship between human activity (roads) and wildfire proximity, which might provide insights into forest cover based on fire susceptibility and human influence.\n\ndf['Road_Fire_Dist_Ratio'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type5' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type5' contains no variation (all zero values). This feature provides no useful information to the model and increases computation cost.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type5'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type14' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type14' also contains no variation (all zero values). Such features do not contribute to model performance and should be removed.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type14'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type37' is dropped\n# Reason: Similar to 'Soil_Type5' and 'Soil_Type14', 'Soil_Type37' contains no variation (all zero values). Removing such features reduces noise in the dataset.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type37'], inplace=True)\nend", "python\n# Step 1: Elevation_Normalized_To_Roadways\n# Feature description: Normalize Elevation by dividing it by Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways. This measures elevation relative to human infrastructure proximity, providing insight into elevation preferences for forest cover near roads.\n# Usefulness: Forest cover type might vary based on elevation in areas closer or farther from roads, as infrastructure affects water drainage, soil quality, and sunlight exposure.\n\ndf['Elevation_Normalized_To_Roadways'] = df['Elevation'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\npython\n# Step 2: Hydrology_Fire_Distance_Diff\n# Feature description: Compute the absolute difference between Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology and Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points. This captures how proximity to water and fire ignition sources differ spatially.\n# Usefulness: Forest cover type might be influenced by areas closer to water for moisture or farther from fire points for reduced fire risk.\n\ndf['Hydrology_Fire_Distance_Diff'] = abs(df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] - df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'])\nend\n\npython\n# Step 3: Soil_Wilderness_Interaction\n# Feature description: Aggregate the sum of all wilderness area indicators and soil type indicators into one feature. This represents a combined relationship between wilderness and soil properties.\n# Usefulness: Different soil types and wilderness areas have distinct ecological characteristics that influence forest cover type.\n\ndf['Soil_Wilderness_Interaction'] = df[['Wilderness_Area1', 'Wilderness_Area2', 'Wilderness_Area3', 'Wilderness_Area4']].sum(axis=1) + df[[col for col in df.columns if 'Soil_Type' in col]].sum(axis=1)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type1' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type1' contains no variation (all zero values). Removing it reduces computation without loss of predictive power.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type1'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type37' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type37' contains no variation (all zero values). Keeping it adds no predictive value and increases dataset noise.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type37'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type15' is dropped\n# Reason: 'Soil_Type15' contains no variation (all zero values), offering no information for predicting forest cover type. Dropping ensures cleaner data.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type15'], inplace=True)\nend", "python\n# Feature: Elevation_To_Hydrology_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Elevation to Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology. This provides a measure of relative elevation compared to water proximity, capturing hydrological and elevation gradients.\n# Usefulness: Forest cover may depend on elevation relative to proximity to water sources, influencing both soil moisture and vegetation type.\ndf['Elevation_To_Hydrology_Ratio'] = df['Elevation'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] + 1e-6)  # Added small constant to prevent division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: Hillshade_Intensity_Range\n# Feature description: Compute the range (difference between max and min) of Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, and Hillshade_3pm. This captures variability in sunlight exposure throughout the day.\n# Usefulness: Certain forest cover types may thrive in areas with stable or highly variable sunlight, making this range a useful metric.\ndf['Hillshade_Intensity_Range'] = df[['Hillshade_9am', 'Hillshade_Noon', 'Hillshade_3pm']].max(axis=1) - df[['Hillshade_9am', 'Hillshade_Noon', 'Hillshade_3pm']].min(axis=1)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: Proximity_Factor_Avg\n# Feature description: Calculate the average of Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways, Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology, and Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points to summarize proximity to anthropogenic and environmental influences.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures combined proximity factors that might impact forest types, balancing environmental and human factors into a single metric.\ndf['Proximity_Factor_Avg'] = (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] + df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] + df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points']) / 3\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type8' is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type8 has minimal variance and mean occurrence (Mean = 0.0003, Std = 0.0186), providing no meaningful predictive value for forest cover type.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type36' is dropped\n# Reason: Soil_Type36 has negligible occurrence (Mean = 0.0001, Std = 0.0103) and lacks variability, making it irrelevant for model training or predictions.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n